# Quadrilaterals and Polygons: Many-Sided Geometry

> Triangles are geometry's atoms; polygons are its molecules. From
> the quadrilaterals of doors, windows, and tiles to the pentagons
> and hexagons of nature and architecture, closed straight-edged
> shapes follow elegant counting rules — and a hierarchy that every
> exam loves to test.

## 1. What Is a Polygon?

[definition]
A **polygon** is a closed plane figure made of straight line
segments. In a **regular** polygon, all sides and all angles are
equal.
[/definition]

| Name | Sides | Interior angle (regular) | Where you meet it |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Triangle | 3 | 60° | Roof trusses |
| Quadrilateral | 4 | 90° (square) | Paper, tiles |
| Pentagon | 5 | 108° | The Pentagon, okra cuts |
| Hexagon | 6 | 120° | Honeycombs, bolts |
| Octagon | 8 | 135° | Stop signs |
| Decagon | 10 | 144° | Coins, mirrors |

## 2. The Angle Formulas

For a polygon with $n$ sides:

@math label="eq:angle-sum"
\text{Interior angle sum} = (n - 2) \times 180°
@/math

@math label="eq:exterior-sum"
\text{Sum of exterior angles} = 360° \quad (\text{always, any polygon})
@/math

[important]
Why $(n-2) \times 180°$? Pick one vertex and draw all diagonals from
it: the polygon splits into exactly $n - 2$ triangles, each
contributing 180°. A quadrilateral gives 2 triangles (360°); a
pentagon gives 3 (540°); a hexagon gives 4 (720°).
[/important]

**Regular polygons.** Each interior angle of a regular $n$-gon:

@math label="eq:regular-interior"
\text{Each interior angle} = \frac{(n-2) \times 180°}{n}
@/math

A regular hexagon: $\frac{4 \times 180°}{6} = 120°$ each. The
matching exterior angle is $\frac{360°}{n}$ — 60° for the hexagon,
which is why hexagons tile so neatly with three meeting at every
point ($3 \times 120° = 360°$).

@chart bar title="Interior angle sum by number of sides"
labels:
Triangle
Quadrilateral
Pentagon
Hexagon
Octagon

data:
180
360
540
720
1080
@/chart

## 3. The Quadrilateral Family Tree

Every quadrilateral has angle sum 360°, but special properties nest
neatly:

@diagram tree id="quad-hierarchy" layout="tree" aria="The quadrilateral family hierarchy"
  "Quadrilateral" -> "Parallelogram"
  "Quadrilateral" -> "Trapezium"
  "Quadrilateral" -> "Kite"
  "Parallelogram" -> "Rectangle"
  "Parallelogram" -> "Rhombus"
  "Rectangle" -> "Square"
  "Rhombus" -> "Square"
@/diagram

| Shape | Sides | Angles | Diagonals |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Parallelogram | Opposite sides equal & parallel | Opposite angles equal | Bisect each other |
| Rectangle | All angles 90° | — | Equal, bisect each other |
| Rhombus | All sides equal | Diagonals bisect angles | Perpendicular bisectors |
| Square | All sides equal | All 90° | Equal, perpendicular, bisect angles |
| Kite | Two pairs of adjacent equal sides | One pair of equal opposite angles | One diagonal bisects the other at 90° |
| Trapezium | One pair of parallel sides | — | — |

[tip]
A square is simultaneously a rectangle and a rhombus — it inherits
every property of both. In exams, "a rhombus with equal diagonals" or
"a rectangle with perpendicular diagonals" both quietly describe a
square.
[/tip]

### Key properties worth memorising

- **Parallelogram** — diagonals bisect each other; each diagonal
  splits it into two congruent triangles.
- **Rectangle** — diagonals are equal: half a rectangle plus its
  diagonal gives the 5-12-13 right triangle.
- **Rhombus** — diagonals are perpendicular bisectors; area =
  half the product of diagonals.
- **Midpoint theorem:** joining midpoints of any quadrilateral in
  order always yields a **parallelogram** (Varignon's theorem).

## 4. Areas

@math label="eq:areas"
\text{Parallelogram} = b \times h, \quad \text{Trapezium} = \tfrac{1}{2}(a + b)h
@/math

@math label="eq:more-areas"
\text{Rhombus} = \tfrac{1}{2}d_1 d_2, \quad \text{Kite} = \tfrac{1}{2}d_1 d_2
@/math

**Worked example.** A rhombus has diagonals 10 cm and 8 cm. Its area
is $\frac{1}{2}(10)(8) = 40$ cm², and each side (half-diagonals 5
and 4 with the right angle between) is $\sqrt{5^2+4^2} = \sqrt{61}$
cm ≈ 7.8 cm.

## 5. Tiling, Nature, and Design

- Only **triangles, quadrilaterals, and hexagons** tile the plane
  edge-to-edge with a single regular shape — which is why bees build
  hexagons: most honey stored for least wax.
- Footballs stitch pentagons (12) with hexagons (20) into a sphere —
  Euler's formula $V - E + F = 2$ counts it exactly.
- Paving, wallpaper, brick bonds, and kirigami all exploit
  quadrilateral geometry: parallelogram tiles interlock and resist
  shifting.

## 6. Concave, Convex, and Diagonals

- **Convex polygon:** every interior angle < 180°; diagonals stay
  inside.
- **Concave polygon:** at least one reflex angle; at least one
  diagonal lies outside (think of an arrowhead).
- A polygon with $n$ sides has $\frac{n(n-3)}{2}$ diagonals: a
  decagon has $\frac{10 \times 7}{2} = 35$.

[summary]
Polygons are closed straight-edged figures; their interior angles sum
to $(n-2) \times 180°$, while exterior angles always sum to 360°.
The quadrilateral family nests by properties: parallelograms
(diagonals bisect), rectangles (equal diagonals), rhombi
(perpendicular diagonals), squares (all of it), plus kites and
trapeziums. Areas follow a few compact formulas, including
rhombus = ½d₁d₂. Hexagons tile with least perimeter, pentagons and
hexagons close into spheres, and every quadrilateral hides a
parallelogram at its midpoints.
[/summary]
